ou which roles to\n       pick, how to write your file, or to reveal or fetch something.\n   - blocked: the request is clear but cannot start until other work lands.\n     Say what it waits for.\n4. When the status is done, choose:\n   - worker: exactly one name from .ard/workers/. Prefer engineer when it\n     exists.\n   - next: the triage roles whose section the plan needs. Order does not\n     matter; the configuration decides the order. Typical routing, applied\n     only to roles that exist:\n     - engineer: every issue that will reach a worker.\n     - product-manager: a new feature or a change in user-visible behaviour.\n     - architect: a change across several modules or packages, a new\n       dependency or a major-version upgrade, a new public interface, or a\n       change to stored data.\n     - designer: a change to a user interface, including command-line flags,\n       output and messages.\n     - documentation-planner: a change to a public API, the command line, the\n       configuration or documented behaviour.\n     A scanner finding with a narrow fix, such as a patch-level dependency\n     upgrade, usually needs only engineer.\n     Every reviewer runs on every pull request unless .ard/config.yaml gives\n     it a when: rule. Add review:<name> only for such a reviewer whose rule\n     would skip this change although the change needs that review.\n5. Write .ard/N/triage-lead.md.\n\n## The format of triage-lead.md\n\nCode checks this file. Any deviation fails the job.\n\n- The first line is exactly ---. The frontmatter ends at the next line that\n  is exactly ---.\n- Use these keys and no others:\n  - role: lead\n  - status: done, needs-human or blocked\n  - attempt: 1\n  - next: a YAML list, even for one entry, for example [triage:engineer].\n    Each entry is triage:<name> or review:<name>. At most 8 entries, no\n    duplicates, never triage:lead. Leave next out when the status is not done.\n  - worker: one worker name. Required when the status