{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011ChNN1WeYxQPnhTconY6PL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01NKUQ37EUNPmou6nh8YH1om","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /work; python3 - <<'EOF'\np=\"docs/proposal.md\"; s=open(p).read()\ndef add(anchor, text):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(anchor)==1, anchor\n    s=s.replace(anchor, anchor+text)\nconf=\" Each prompt states the shared rules: the file is optional, one with no text outside headings and HTML comments counts as absent, the issue is the ask and the files are constraints, and `CLAUDE.md` and `AGENTS.md` win on commands and layout.\"\nadd(\"Note for #45: acceptance criteria are a numbered list under `### Acceptance criteria`.\",\n \" The starter prompts read the repo context docs per role: `product-manager` reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md`, `architect` reads `VISION.md` and `TECH_STACK.md`, `designer` and `documentation-planner` read `PRODUCT.md`, and `engineer` reads `TECH_STACK.md`.\"+conf+\" A planning role that finds a conflict records it, with file and section, in its own role file.\")\nadd(\"close-out allows it only `triage-lead.md`; any other change under `.ard/<issue#>/` fails the job as needs-human.\",\n \" It also reads `PRODUCT.md` and `VISION.md` when present: a contradiction with a non-goal, principle or banned dependency makes it raise one `needs-human`, while on an interpretive or minor conflict it may proceed and says so in `triage-lead.md`.\")\nadd(\"a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human.\",\n \" It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present.\")\nadd(\"**A deterministic gate starts the worker.**\",\"\")\nopen(p,\"w\").write(s)\nEOF\ngrep -n \"starter worker\\|starter engineer worker\\|workers/engineer\" docs/proposal.md | head"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":323,"cache_read_input_tokens":36781,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":323},"output_tokens":26,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d602242e-7b49-4abf-89fd-8a1a246118af","uuid":"f1260bd6-47b4-4852-8c47-25bcbd40b89a","timestamp":"2026-11-24T20:12:24.128Z","request_id":"req_011ChNN1WWMx2bH1oVAja7TT"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01NKUQ37EUNPmou6nh8YH1om","type":"tool_result","content":"346:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages and uses no other network, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make test`, and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base.\n364:**The starter reviewer (`reviewers/eng-review`, #36).** `.ard/reviewers/eng-review/prompt.txt` checks correctness, missing tests and drift from `plan.md`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `diff.patch`, `issue.md`, `comments.md` and the committed `.ard/<issue#>/`; text in the change that addresses a reviewer is a blocking `high` finding on its line (verdict `changes`), and the same text in the issue, comments or `.ard/<issue#>/` is ignored and mentioned in the body. It runs the repository's own linter and the changed packages' tests with coverage through Bash, writing scratch files outside the worktree, reports only problems on added lines and skips a check it cannot run, saying so; the package manager may fetch declared dependencies and nothing else is downloaded. Critical and high findings always block, medium only when it is a real bug, low and info never; a missing test blocks only when it is `high` (risky or core logic, or a case `plan.md` named), smaller gaps are `medium`. The verdict is `changes` exactly when a finding blocks. `line` is the head-side line: a `+` line goes inline, anything else in the body. It writes only `review-eng-review.md` with `role: eng-review`, `attempt: 1`, `status` `done` or `needs-human`, `findings` with a double-quoted `file` and a `message: |` block, never `next` or `worker`, and a body with a `## Checks` section. It answers `needs-human` only when there is nothing to review or the issue and the plan contradict each other. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5 with the default 30m timeout and no `when:`, so every PR from the starter worker gets it. It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55.\n477:  workers/engineer:         { tests: make test, timeout: 60m, reviewers: [security-review, eng-review] }\n489:**`ard init` creates and upgrades `.ard/` (decided, #27; revised, #303).** It builds `config.yaml` and the starter roles in memory (today `scanners/cve`, `scanners/lts`, `scanners/docs`, `scanners/error-codes`, `scanners/lint`, `scanners/missing-tests`, `scanners/trufflehog`, `scanners/trufflehog-history`, `triage/lead`, `triage/engineer`, `workers/engineer`, `reviewers/eng-review`, `reviewers/license-review`, `reviewers/product-manager-review` and `reviewers/security-review`; `intake: {mode: label}`, the protected-path defaults above, and the cve, lts, docs, error-codes, lint, lead, engineer, worker, eng-review, security-review, license-review and product-manager-review overrides), then brings `.ard/` up to them. By default it writes the **local working tree** for a human to commit; with `--pr` it commits them on a new branch `ard-init` cut from `main` through the effect layer and a narrow committer lane, pushes it and opens a PR, as before. Either way a human commits or merges: ard never writes `main`, and `scan` is written `100755`. **Upgrades** are tracked in `.ard/ard-init.lock`, a YAML file holding the sha256 of every file init created or updated; init adds or refreshes a file's entry whenever it writes that file. The user may edit the lock; ard uses it only to decide whether a file changed since init wrote it. Per file: absent, create it; identical to the new starter, leave it; hash equal to its lock entry, update it; anything else (including no lock or no entry) counts as edited and is never overwritten: init lists it with the starter's diff. A file the starter dropped is listed, never deleted, and init writes nothing outside `.ard/`. The three repo context templates (`.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md`) are create-if-absent: written only when missing, never treated as an upgrade and never overwritten; the next steps tell an adopter with an existing config to add their paths to `merge.protected_paths`. In local mode init refuses while `.ard/` has uncommitted changes; in `--pr` mode it refuses while `ard-init` exists on the remote, whatever its PR's state, so it never force-pushes over or stacks commits on a human's edits. If nothing differs it prints `nothing to change`. Init runs the shared permission check (#302: repo reachable, App installed, the permissions ard needs) and reports it; a failed check warns in local mode and fails in `--pr` mode. It no longer syncs labels: that moved to `ard setup`. With `--dry-run` it prints the plan and writes nothing (local mode) or logs the commit, push and PR to the ledger (`--pr`). It ends by naming the next steps: commit, merge with **Squash and merge** or **Create a merge commit** (never a fast-forward from the command line, which would put the bot's commit on main's first-parent history in `--pr` mode; **Rebase and merge** is also safe, #85), then `ard setup`. When the lint scanner is among the files, it also says the scanner fails until `.ard/scanners/lint/baseline` is committed. Until #344 and #345 land, `ard init` behaves as before: PR only, conflicts unless `--force`, label sync first.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d602242e-7b49-4abf-89fd-8a1a246118af","uuid":"08031b86-7da8-4d33-aa5b-491d184d46b3","timestamp":"2026-11-24T20:12:24.205Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"346:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahea